#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097407} :activity/id #uuid "ef8b70d7-53d9-5368-870b-fb3c6b4e76be" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430 :topic/id #uuid "9eb245aa-f88f-5f14-8f09-1f18ad67f8ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1076} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097811 :tutorial/content "The trigonometric expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\sin( x + {\\color{red}\\phi})\n$$\n\nis called the **amplitude-phase form** of sine. Here, ${\\color{blue}R}$ is the **amplitude** and ${\\color{red}\\phi}$ is the **phase** of the sine function.\n\nThe sum or difference of a sine function and a cosine function with the same argument can always be written as a sine function in amplitude-phase form. That is to say, if $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\sin x \\pm b \\cos x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\nR \\sin(x \\pm \\phi).\n$$\n\nfor some $R>0$ and $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "69964ee4-693c-5a43-af92-88232e3d1471" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2572 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "88224b15-9a6d-5755-a5b2-af292977df30" :step/math-academy-id 11889 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097812 :tutorial/content "We've been using the amplitude-phase form for sine, but there is also an amplitude-phase form for cosine. If $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\cos x \\pm b \\sin x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\cos(x \\mp {\\color{red}\\phi} )\n$$\n\nfor some ${\\color{blue}R}>0$ and ${\\color{red}\\phi} \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$ \n\nNote that the sign in the last formula flips due to the identity $\\cos(x \\mp \\phi) = \\cos x \\cos \\phi \\pm \\sin x \\sin \\phi,$ which reverses the sign when expanding.\n\nWriting this more explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\operatorname{cos}x+b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ a\\operatorname{cos}x-b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's write the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sin{x}+\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form with cosine. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi.$ Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the first equation by the second one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(4) \\\\  & \\approx 1.326\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}ϕ+{cos}^{2}ϕ) & =16+1 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =17 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =\\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\operatorname{arctan}(4)) \\\\  & \\approx \\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-1.326)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3bb9db1-8b71-5f57-8cd5-5a113632e7d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2574 :tutorial/title "The Amplitude-Phase Form For Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "d14d2ddb-2f8f-5f82-8559-27101b7b4ca4" :step/math-academy-id 11921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097412}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096606 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096406 :question/id #uuid "6010c0f3-67d3-5b21-af26-695a864fb42a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3541"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478465}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "3819f187-e5ca-5e24-8560-2b9695d3ec9d" :step/math-academy-id 11924 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097411}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096607 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096407 :question/id #uuid "91e6008e-5c37-55aa-8cf6-cac61198f459" :question/math-academy-id "e-3542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049773}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375398}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "f1336a15-53ea-5387-ae8a-f35eef5dcb59" :step/math-academy-id 11925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097409}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096608 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096408 :question/id #uuid "557b1b6e-c54a-5fa9-85e1-3cea346c3bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478395}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "6c1dd27c-aafa-514b-8675-0cf06d6d9413" :step/math-academy-id 11928 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096609 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096409 :question/id #uuid "9ea36397-ab9d-5567-8b38-3e83ba624e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3544"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478456}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "a827daaf-41a5-5e6d-be23-699162ae1f94" :step/math-academy-id 11929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097825 :tutorial/content "Let's write the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi$. Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}ϕ+{sin}^{2}ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x} = {\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{3}} \\sin \\left(x + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}} \\right)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81c76ef1-c149-53cf-a9e2-d93a243a7002" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2887 :tutorial/title "Writing a Sum or Difference of Sine and Cosine Using Amplitude-Phase Form"} :step/id #uuid "d77693ed-4059-5f7a-88e9-25300c5994e9" :step/math-academy-id 15506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097410}}] :activity/title "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096867}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406582} :activity/id #uuid "e1be1a0c-7a7b-5288-bec9-333df360e987" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430 :topic/id #uuid "9eb245aa-f88f-5f14-8f09-1f18ad67f8ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1076} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f6be78-4a73-582f-b9be-ccd54037b7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406560} {:answer/id #uuid "4493693b-c02e-5ba8-9c1f-0e602a8b0eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406562} {:answer/id #uuid "9d920b62-e442-5621-8fa9-46a7ba4002db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406565} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a70056-20f6-5d8c-bd45-fe08a274eba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406569} {:answer/id #uuid "dac58597-74ad-5c55-82b7-a592a5d0742b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406562} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1825133-7014-5a09-9154-148509062f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79089117-3e5a-51ff-ae41-40a7e2901f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65578" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a06f20a6-d2e4-56ba-a165-46b7fcfbdf95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008d940c-e694-5cf3-8295-360b6cad867c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406559} {:answer/id #uuid "bf267273-4455-5b6e-9339-b99f0be93f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406567} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c281e4-9095-5e8a-ba0f-aabb9383d4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406568} {:answer/id #uuid "d474685d-0b20-52ae-90c4-81e995b0ce85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406570} {:answer/id #uuid "f607a11b-899b-59ab-b189-957fb6465093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406570} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4d9909-2b00-5980-9b7e-95f491417da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "139292f5-27c6-5b2f-8f13-1b15372d0a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65581" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =3 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cc02456f-a095-5e41-95ce-6a946f125439"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283fcbb3-8865-526f-9a34-cc2afbcdadd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406561} {:answer/id #uuid "641d46cb-ca02-51d3-a8b9-699328a21c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406563} {:answer/id #uuid "709cd0f7-451f-5ee9-8074-0e603e78ea3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406564} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a219de-60fc-550f-9a26-7578926916c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406566} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0bccfb-38e4-51f1-8863-481369f3db5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406573}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406561} :answer-field/id #uuid "83d8f167-e779-56fd-836e-ab6aac4f2070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e58d0138-9eca-5984-8b18-90ecf02f0d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11294" :question/problem "$3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =18 \\\\ R & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe07e852-ca7d-55ad-9fb8-dab24d09b6c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406580}}] :activity/title "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406558}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478480} :activity/id #uuid "1bec84e2-1043-5e8e-b964-1d7ab8860e62" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430 :topic/id #uuid "9eb245aa-f88f-5f14-8f09-1f18ad67f8ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1076} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478471 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a826e3-1f4a-56c0-a4f6-8617344946df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477940}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477940} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c664a42-d4ca-5edc-9480-a51aeced73f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fb921c8c-f3ea-56c4-a4fb-c6ad860350a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-274555" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =60\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0dbd6ed9-902e-5580-9860-40bf52b3cfca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ed6854a-cf74-591e-8fe2-00619854ab94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477853} {:answer/id #uuid "454d32d4-5e30-5d47-9068-bb3220f00149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477866} {:answer/id #uuid "8a107828-920e-532b-946a-234e69c84613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478015} {:answer/id #uuid "9788048e-f8b7-5586-af01-ee2107752378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478031} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a20e78-4df7-5e41-99a4-c960fe582b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478015} :answer-field/id #uuid "78daaf88-e999-5da1-8bf1-f099b8191645" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e5e4ccfa-82ff-5c85-afb9-eb5972468216" :question/math-academy-id "q-30579" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =1 \\\\ R & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "78630075-227a-5ca0-828c-0976fac97170" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5b735d-63dc-59e6-b41c-77003c0e40a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333398} {:answer/id #uuid "57318f06-cd50-578b-ae0b-794fe204ec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333782} {:answer/id #uuid "575d47bf-3cbd-57ee-92d6-60684fb5f9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333788} {:answer/id #uuid "688f9863-4f33-5417-9de0-c09ccf5fcb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333949} {:answer/id #uuid "a0da0bdd-42a6-5ec1-a897-867603b5eeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334401}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334401} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d64f22-bb75-503b-b5b4-1ad2bd5bcbcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e0c19689-c107-5415-8074-e35dadeefb3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65582" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "95c82cdb-7880-558f-88e9-b94422898315" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c19d84af-2e7c-52ec-9fb2-1c4d02dfc257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478113} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a184adc-97c3-5fd4-aa16-2ab651c506af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dc2a00c1-69ff-5ca9-838c-e411473b3c5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-327994" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(4)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"} :step/id #uuid "a34e8a35-52b5-58c8-9bea-d76482bdc8bf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b22226-96da-5ac3-837f-85c6d962014a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.927)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477761} {:answer/id #uuid "23b112a7-45d9-59f5-b11b-4a043ad38f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.927)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477806} {:answer/id #uuid "d040139f-5db2-5449-b5ff-e7cc5e152405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.644)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478148} {:answer/id #uuid "dccbee6a-dd05-5649-ad87-d2d6be5453e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.644)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478168} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0972a5-aa0a-506b-9f2d-6cb313e5a657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477806} :answer-field/id #uuid "efe9231d-0800-5ee8-8509-d4d9ca580e2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41ce9e80-1405-5642-831b-08af7169731b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107575" :question/problem "$3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.927)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e35f850c-10ed-539a-bd7c-5ad04970de53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478471}}] :activity/title "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477728}]}
